---
import Base from "~/layouts/Base.astro";
import Icon from "~/components/Icon.astro";
import StatusTag from "~/components/StatusTag.astro";

const PAPER = "https://hal.science/hal-05193465v1";
const SPEC = "https://github.com/brig-id/spec/blob/main";

const authors =
  "Fanny Georges, Antoine Seilles, Guillaume Artignan, Bérenger Arnaud, Mountaz Hascoët, Nancy Rodriguez, Birgitta Dresp-Langley, Jean Sallantin";

interface Entry {
  date: string;
  datetime: string;
  title: string;
  text: string;
  link?: { label: string; href: string };
}

const built: Entry[] = [
  {
    date: "24 May 2026",
    datetime: "2026-05-24",
    title: "First commit",
    text: "The cryptography library, the core, the server and the specifications are started on the same day. The specifications come first on purpose: the system is meant to be audited.",
  },
  {
    date: "29 July 2026",
    datetime: "2026-07-29",
    title: "A face",
    text: "The web app begins: sign-up, sign-in and passkey management in a browser.",
  },
  {
    date: "4 September 2026",
    datetime: "2026-09-04",
    title: "leaf 0.1.0",
    text: "First release of the single-server edition, as a container image. In the same days the project's own server, helm.brig.id, goes online.",
    link: {
      label: "Releases",
      href: "https://github.com/brig-id/server-leaf/releases",
    },
  },
  {
    date: "10 November 2026",
    datetime: "2026-11-10",
    title: "Accounts and identities part ways",
    text: "The decision that gives brig·id its shape: an account is private and never shown to a website; an identity is what a website meets. Until then, one record played both roles.",
    link: {
      label: "ADR-002",
      href: `${SPEC}/decisions/002-accounts-and-identities.md`,
    },
  },
  {
    date: "11 November 2026",
    datetime: "2026-11-11",
    title: "leaf 0.2 and the next decisions",
    text: "Identities reach a release. The same day settles how sessions work, how a website will obtain an identity, and what its identifier is made of.",
    link: { label: "Decisions", href: `${SPEC}/decisions` },
  },
];

const next = [
  "A recovery code, for the day every passkey is lost",
  "Sign-up in one gesture, with no username to invent",
  "Sign-in to websites with the identity you choose",
];
---

<Base
  title="History"
  description="From a 2009 research paper on the semiotics of digital identity to a self-hosted identity server: where brig·id comes from."
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="shell-narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">History</p>
      <h1>
        From a paper to a <span class="accent spectrum">server.</span>
      </h1>
      <p class="lead">
        brig·id is young, and its idea is not. It took seventeen years, and a
        few technologies that did not exist yet, to get from one to the other.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell-narrow chapter">
      <p class="year rise">2009</p>
      <div class="prose rise">
        <h2>A study of two social networks</h2>
        <p>
          In 2009, eight researchers publish a paper at the H2PTM conference,
          with Fanny Georges as first author. Its title, translated:{" "}
          <strong>
            Semiotics and visualisation of digital identity: a comparative study
            of Facebook and MySpace.
          </strong>
        </p>
        <p>
          The paper looks at how the interfaces of the web of that time shape
          the way a person is represented, and at what happens when an identity
          is turned into numbers and charts. It reads a profile in three layers:
        </p>
      </div>

      <ul class="layers rise">
        <li>
          <b>Declarative</b>
          <span>
            what the person enters: a name, a photograph, a description.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Acting</b>
          <span>what the platform reports the person doing.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Calculated</b>
          <span>
            what the platform computes and displays: counts, totals, rankings.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <div class="prose rise">
        <p>
          Only the first layer is written by the person. The other two are
          written by the platform, and they are the ones that grow.
        </p>
      </div>

      <figure class="cite card hue-self rise">
        <blockquote lang="fr">
          Sémiotique et visualisation de l’identité numérique&nbsp;: une étude
          comparée de Facebook et Myspace
        </blockquote>
        <figcaption>
          {authors}. <cite>H2PTM 2009</cite>.
        </figcaption>
        <a class="link-arrow" href={PAPER}>
          Read it on HAL
          <Icon name="arrow-up-right" />
        </a>
      </figure>

      <div class="prose rise">
        <p>
          One of the eight is Bérenger Arnaud, who would go on to start brig·id.
          What stayed with him from that work is a plain idea: people do not
          have one digital identity but several, and the more a system computes
          about them, the less of each identity is theirs to write.
        </p>
        <p>
          The paper studies social networks. It does not describe brig·id, and
          its other authors have no part in this project.
          <a href="https://berenger.arnaud.work/research">
            More about that research.
          </a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell-narrow chapter">
      <p class="year rise">2009 → 2026</p>
      <div class="prose rise">
        <h2>Why it took until now</h2>
        <p>
          For most of those years the idea had no practical form. Signing in
          meant a password, and a separate identity meant one more password to
          forget. Two things changed that.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Passkeys.</strong> Since 2022, the main browsers and
            operating systems let people sign in with a gesture instead of a
            secret they must remember. One strong, private way in became
            realistic.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Post-quantum standards.</strong> In August 2024, NIST
            published FIPS 203 and FIPS 204. An identity system started today
            can be built on cryptography meant to outlast it.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Meanwhile, the sign-in buttons of a few large platforms became the
          default way to enter a website: the single account, everywhere, that
          the 2009 paper could only glimpse.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell-narrow chapter">
      <p class="year rise">2026</p>
      <div class="prose rise">
        <h2>Building it</h2>
      </div>

      <ol class="timeline">
        {built.map((entry) => (
          <li class="rise">
            <time datetime={entry.datetime}>{entry.date}</time>
            <h3>{entry.title}</h3>
            <p>{entry.text}</p>
            {entry.link && (
              <a class="link-arrow" href={entry.link.href}>
                {entry.link.label}
                <Icon name="arrow-up-right" />
              </a>
            )}
          </li>
        ))}
        <li class="rise upcoming">
          <StatusTag status="progress" />
          <h3>Next</h3>
          <ul>
            {next.map((item) => (
              <li>{item}</li>
            ))}
          </ul>
          <a class="link-arrow" href="/features/">
            The status of everything
            <Icon name="arrow-right" />
          </a>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell-narrow chapter">
      <p class="year rise">A note</p>
      <div class="prose rise">
        <h2>On the names</h2>
        <p>
          The parts of brig·id are named after a tree. <code>roots</code> is the
          workspace everything grows from. <code>leaf</code> is a single server,{" "}
          <code>grove</code> will be several working as one, and
          <code>forest</code> the largest deployments. The first server is
          called <code>helm</code>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .chapter {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1.75rem;
  }

  .year {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-style: italic;
    font-size: clamp(3.4rem, 11vw, 6.5rem);
    line-height: 0.9;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    background: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--self), rgb(255 243 223 / 0.15));
    -webkit-background-clip: text;
    background-clip: text;
    color: transparent;
  }

  .layers {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .layers li {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 8.5rem 1fr;
    gap: 1rem;
    padding: 0.9rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    color: var(--text-2);
  }

  .layers b {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.76rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    line-height: 2.1;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .cite {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1.1rem;
    margin: 0.5rem 0;
    padding: clamp(1.5rem, 4vw, 2.4rem);
    background:
      radial-gradient(
        30rem 16rem at 100% 0%,
        rgb(255 243 223 / 0.08),
        transparent 70%
      ),
      var(--bg-1);
  }

  .cite blockquote {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-style: italic;
    font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 3.4vw, 2.2rem);
    line-height: 1.2;
    color: var(--self);
  }

  .cite figcaption {
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--text-2);
  }

  .cite cite {
    font-style: normal;
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .timeline {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0;
    margin-left: 0.35rem;
    border-left: 1px solid var(--line-2);
  }

  .timeline > li {
    position: relative;
    display: grid;
    justify-items: start;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    padding: 0 0 2.5rem 2rem;
  }

  .timeline > li::before {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    left: -0.36rem;
    top: 0.45rem;
    width: 0.7rem;
    height: 0.7rem;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--self);
    box-shadow:
      0 0 0 0.3rem var(--bg),
      0 0 1rem var(--amber);
  }

  .timeline > li.upcoming::before {
    background: var(--bg);
    box-shadow:
      0 0 0 1px var(--amber) inset,
      0 0 0 0.3rem var(--bg);
  }

  .timeline time {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.76rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text-3);
  }

  .timeline h3 {
    font-size: 1.4rem;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }

  .timeline p {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--text-2);
  }

  .timeline .link-arrow {
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: var(--text-2);
  }

  .upcoming ul {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.4rem;
    padding-left: 1.1rem;
    list-style: disc;
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    color: var(--text-2);
  }

  @media (max-width: 36rem) {
    .layers li {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 0;
    }
  }
</style>
